<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Jassa Singh Ahluwalia</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1718–1783</span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Jassa Singh Ahluwalia was a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sikhism">Sikh</a> leader who became supreme commander of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dal-khalsa">Dal Khalsa</a>, the combined Sikh forces, from 1748, and head of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ahluwalia-misl">Ahluwalia misl</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sikhs">Sikhs</a> fought the Mughal governors and the invasions of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ahmad-shah-abdali">Ahmad Shah Durrani</a> under his leadership, and he briefly held <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lahore">Lahore</a> in 1761, where he struck coins. He took Kapurthala in the 1770s, and the Ahluwalia family ruled Kapurthala State until 1947.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/jassa-singh-ahluwalia.jpg" alt="Jassa Singh Ahluwalia" width="320" height="402" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown authorUnknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Equestrian_painting_of_Jassa_Singh_Ahluwalia_on_horseback_in-front_of_his_haveli_with_a_fly-whisk_attendant,_circa_late_18th_century.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
